    "id": "amoebic-colitis",
    "condition": "Amoebic colitis (intestinal amoebiasis)",
    "dasChapter": "Ch. 34 — Examination of chronic abdominal conditions",
    "region": "ABDOMEN",
    "status": "cited",
    "synonyms": [
      "amoebic colitis",
      "amebic colitis",
      "amoebic dysentery",
      "intestinal amoebiasis",
      "chronic diarrhoea blood mucus tropical",
      "amoebic dysentery chronic",
      "flask shaped ulcer colon",
      "amoeboma",
      "colonic mass amoebic",
      "colonic mass mimicking carcinoma amoebic"
    ],
    "redFlags": [
      "Fulminant amoebic colitis with toxic megacolon or perforation is rare but carries a high mortality and needs urgent recognition",
      "Amoeboma (colonic mass mimicking carcinoma) — a mass lesion caused by the infection — can be mistaken for a colonic carcinoma, and the reverse mistake happens too",
      "Extraintestinal spread, chiefly to the liver, can occur without current diarrhoea, so its absence does not exclude the diagnosis",
      "In a patient being treated for presumed ulcerative colitis who does not respond as expected, this diagnosis is reconsidered rather than the steroid dose simply increased — steroids given for unrecognised amoebic disease can worsen it seriously"
    ],
    "algorithm": [
      {
        "id": "a1",
        "stage": "Presentation",
        "title": "Chronic or recurrent diarrhoea, often with blood and mucus",
        "detail": "The course can be intermittent over months, mimicking the relapsing pattern of ulcerative colitis, or it can be an acute dysenteric illness with frequent small-volume bloody stools.",
        "next": [
          "a2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "a2",
        "stage": "Context",
        "title": "Residence in or exposure to areas with poor sanitation",
        "detail": "The infection is spread by faecally contaminated food or water. It is common worldwide in areas of poor sanitation, and India carries a significant share of the global disease burden, so in Indian practice this sits high on the differential rather than at the bottom of it.",
        "next": [
          "a3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "a3",
        "stage": "Examination",
        "title": "Often unremarkable, or mild lower abdominal tenderness",
        "detail": "Unlike the systemic upset of severe ulcerative colitis, examination findings can be surprisingly modest even with significant colonic disease.",
        "next": [
          "a4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "a4",
        "stage": "The differential that matters most",
        "title": "Ulcerative colitis looks similar and is treated in the opposite direction",
        "detail": "Both cause chronic bloody mucous diarrhoea with a relapsing course. Confirming or excluding amoebic infection before starting immunosuppressive treatment for presumed ulcerative colitis matters a great deal, because treating unrecognised amoebic colitis with steroids can cause serious deterioration.",
        "branches": [
          {
            "label": "Colitis without confirmed cause",
            "to": "ulcerative-colitis"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "a5",
        "stage": "Investigate",
        "title": "Stool microscopy for the organism, and colonoscopy with biopsy",
        "detail": "Fresh stool examination for the parasite, together with colonoscopic appearance and biopsy showing the characteristic flask-shaped ulcers, establishes the diagnosis. Serology is more useful for extraintestinal disease than for colitis alone.",
        "next": [
          "a6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "a6",
        "stage": "Look beyond the colon",
        "title": "Ask about right upper quadrant pain and fever, which may indicate liver spread",
        "detail": "The infection can spread to the liver even when bowel symptoms have settled or were never prominent, so this question is asked regardless of how the colitis itself is behaving.",
        "branches": [
          {
            "label": "Right upper quadrant pain and fever",
            "to": "amoebic-liver-abscess"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "a7",
        "stage": "Amoeboma",
        "title": "A mass-forming variant that can mimic a colonic tumour",
        "detail": "A chronic granulomatous mass caused by the infection can be indistinguishable clinically and radiologically from carcinoma. Biopsy is what settles the question, and it is pursued rather than assumed either way.",
        "branches": [
          {
            "label": "Colonic mass on imaging or examination",
            "to": "colorectal-carcinoma"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "a8",
        "stage": "Decision",
        "title": "Antiamoebic drug treatment, with a second course to clear luminal cysts",
        "detail": "As with amoebic liver abscess, treating the tissue-invading form alone does not reliably clear gut colonisation, so a luminal agent is added as standard practice.",
        "next": []
      }
    ],
