    "synonyms": [
      "anal fissure",
      "fissure in ano",
      "fissure-in-ano",
      "sentinel pile",
      "painful defaecation with bleeding",
      "streak of blood with stool and pain",
      "posterior anal ulcer",
      "chronic anal ulcer sentinel tag"
    ],
    "redFlags": [
      "A fissure situated off the midline, or multiple fissures, should not be assumed to be the ordinary kind — Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, syphilis, HIV-related disease and malignancy are all associated with atypical fissures and are considered",
      "Extreme sphincter spasm can make digital or proctoscopic examination impossible at the first visit — this is not a reason to skip the diagnosis, only to defer full instrumentation",
      "Chronic fissure with an unusually indurated base or edge is biopsied rather than assumed benign"
    ],
    "algorithm": [
      {
        "id": "f1",
        "stage": "What it is",
        "title": "A longitudinal tear in the anal canal, in the great majority posteriorly in the midline",
        "detail": "Several anatomical explanations are described for the posterior predominance — posterior angulation and relative fixation of the anal canal, the direction of the external sphincter fibres, and the elliptical shape of the canal itself.",
        "next": [
          "f2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f2",
        "stage": "Acute fissure",
        "title": "A deep tear with surrounding oedema and sphincter spasm",
        "detail": "Bright streaked blood with the stool, and pain after defaecation, are the characteristic features. Acute fissures often heal on their own.",
        "next": [
          "f3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f3",
        "stage": "Chronic fissure",
        "title": "A deep, undermined ulcer when the acute fissure fails to heal",
        "detail": "The ulcer stops above at the pectinate line. Below it, a hypertrophied papilla and skin tag — the sentinel pile — is a recognisable marker. Being situated in the lower anal canal, this is intensely painful and associated with marked sphincter spasm.",
        "next": [
          "f4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f4",
        "stage": "Examine gently, and be willing to defer instrumentation",
        "title": "Sphincter spasm can make full examination genuinely impossible at first presentation",
        "detail": "Rectal examination can feel the chronic ulcer, but severe spasm may mean proctoscopy has to wait for a later visit once pain has settled, rather than being forced through on the first attempt.",
        "next": [
          "f5"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f5",
        "stage": "Distinguish from other causes of anal pain and bleeding",
        "title": "Thrombosed external piles, perianal abscess, and malignant ulceration",
        "detail": "Pain confined to a swelling rather than a linear tear points towards thrombosed piles or an abscess. An atypical fissure — off the midline, unusually indurated, or not settling with standard treatment — is a reason to reconsider the diagnosis rather than a reason to keep treating the same way.",
        "branches": [
          {
            "label": "Tender rounded lump, not a linear tear",
            "to": "anorectal-fistula"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f6",
        "stage": "Decision",
        "title": "Conservative management first for most acute and many chronic fissures; procedural treatment for those that fail to heal",
        "detail": "Dietary and stool-softening measures, and topical treatment aimed at relaxing the internal sphincter, are first-line. Fissures resistant to these measures are referred for a surgical opinion; specific procedure choice is a colorectal decision.",
        "next": []
      }
    ],
